  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCold front is currently pushing through South Texas, nearing \nVictoria and Hebronville as of mid-day. Ahead of the front starting \nto see some higher cloud tops develop along the coast with mainly \nshowers so far, but expect thunderstorms as we move through the \nafternoon. SPC MEsoanalysis indicating an area of 2k SB CAPE along \nthe coast at this time, with marginal low to mid-level lapse rates \n(around 6 C/KM). Low level winds and shear are notably less than \nideal for severe storm development, but an isolated stronger storm \napproaching severe levels (hail) is not out of the question. Once \nthe front moves through the severe threat will end. Expect the front \nto reach the coast by 5-7pm. \n\nBehind the front, surface moisture will drop quickly - COT already \nhas dewpoints in the 40s. Temperatures will not drop as drastically \nthough and should remain in the 60s into the evening. Cloud cover \nsticks around tonight to limit radiational cooling, but still expect \nlows into the 50s - and a few locations upper 40s across the area. \n\nMonday looks dry, but some weak isentropic lift developing Tuesday \n(~305K/700mb level) will have enough moisture to work with to bring \nsome showers and possibly a thunderstorms associated with a 500-\n700mb shortwave. Once this wave pushes through it'll usher in the \ncoolest air of the season with highs in the 50s and 60s Tues through \nthe end of the week. Expect lows in the 30s for portions of the \narea, mainly north and inland Wednesday and Thursday. Thanksgiving \nDay looks to have the coolest low, but mainly mid and upper 30s. \nChances for freezing temperatures are very low. If you're traveling \nnorth in Texas though, probabilities increase for seeing briefly \nfreezing temperatures.",
